Previous Lessons:
1. In Alloy 6, function bodies must be a single expression defined with '=' instead of curly braces, and predicate-style bodies are not allowed; use quantified expressions or let-bindings for a single return value.
2. Curly-brace set comprehensions cannot be used as function bodies in Alloy 6; instead, use '=' followed by a single expression or quantifier expression of the declared type.
3. Can't use a quantifier block (e.g., some r: T | ...) as the body of a function in Alloy 6 → Must use a single expression of the declared return type (e.g., Boolean expression).
4. When defining functions in Alloy 6 that must return a single element, use quantifier expressions (e.g., one c: ...) instead of set comprehension blocks, as curly braces in function bodies will yield set-valued expressions and can trigger syntax errors if not used with the correct context.
5. In Alloy 6, function definitions with curly brace bodies must be terminated with a semicolon to prevent syntax and parser errors at subsequent top-level constructs and declarations.

