Previous Lessons:
1. Alloy 6 requires all function declarations to have an explicit return type; omitting the return type causes a syntax error.
2. Alloy doesn't support method-like function application with arguments in all contexts; must use relational join and comprehension.
3. Always specify a return type in Alloy 6 function declarations to prevent syntax errors related to omitted type information.
4. When writing helper functions in Alloy that return logical truth values, do not specify an enum or signature as the return type; let Alloy infer a PrimitiveBoolean result from the formula.
5. Can't return an enum value (e.g., Bool) from a function whose body is a Boolean formula in Alloy 6 → Must omit the return type and allow Alloy to infer a PrimitiveBoolean result for Boolean-valued functions.

